      "evidence": {
        "source": "manual_review",
        "cve_id": null,
        "description": "Resembles SQL injection: two raw request.GET values (status, assignee) are taken straight from query parameters and passed into .filter(status=status) / .filter(assignee_id=assignee), which a scanner may treat as user input flowing into a query. It is actually SAFE: these are Django ORM filter calls, which produce fully parameterized SQL - the values are bound as parameters, never string-concatenated into SQL, so no injection is possible. The queryset also starts from task_queryset_for(request.user), which tenant-scopes results, and the assignee filter is additionally gated behind is_staff_role(request.user). No raw(), extra(), or cursor.execute is used."
      },
